# Ben-Or, Kelmer, Rabin 1994
# "Asynchronous Secure Computations with Optimal Resilience"
# Section 4 Figure 3 - Protocol Agreement[Q]
#
# Paper-faithful capture of the BKR94 Asynchronous Common Subset
# (ACS) protocol's rules.  Identifier and predicate translation,
# and any C-side bridge decisions (sender validation, message
# routing across process/round/initiator, etc.), live in
# bkr94acsToC.dtc.
#
# Composition.  BKR94 ACS is built on top of N reliable-broadcast
# instances (one per process A-Casting a value) and N binary-
# agreement instances BA_j (one per process, deciding whether to
# include that process's A-Cast in the agreed subset).  The
# rules below describe what the ACS layer does in response to
# the events those underlying primitives surface; the primitives
# themselves are specified in bracha87Fig1.dtc (A-Cast) and
# bracha87Fig3.dtc / bracha87Fig4.dtc (BA).
#
# In this deployment Q(j) = 1 means P_j has broadcast a value,
# realized by Bracha87's Figure 1 accepting the reliable
# broadcast of P_j's A-Cast.  Bracha's Lemma 3 makes this
# realization satisfy BKR94's assumption (2) on Q (if any honest
# accepts, every honest eventually accepts), and Lemma 4 makes
# it satisfy assumption (1) (an honest P_j's broadcast is
# accepted at every honest process).  Q is application-
# supplied and BKR94's own MPC construction instantiated it
# differently, so this choice is recorded in bkr94acsToC.dtc and
# is not a paper concern.
#
# The Step 2 trigger is "n-t BA outputs of 1," not "n-t Q(j) = 1
# events".  These coincide in benign runs but diverge under
# asynchrony or Byzantine scheduling; only the BA-output-1
# trigger satisfies Part A case (i) of the BKR94 Lemma 2 proof.
#
# Step 2, like every rule in the paper, is stated in the
# asynchronous model: unbounded (finite) message delay, no clocks
# (BKR94ACS.txt, Model and tools).  Each rule's "upon" names the
# evidence that enables an action; the model has no notion of the
# moment the action is taken, and the Lemma 2 proof is read as asking
# of the fanout only that the 0-inputs are eventually entered once
# the count holds (BPR.md, The Sweep-Side Decisions).
# Encoding Step 2 on the arrival path, firing at the instant the
# local count crosses n-t, reads a moment into a rule that names
# only evidence, and drops the unbounded-latency notion the model
# rests on.  The Step 2 section below records the rule; its firing
# is paced by the caller from the BPR sweep tick, the same tick
# bracha87.[hc] drives BPR from.  Zero patience is not a firing at
# enabling (BPR.md, The Sweep-Side Decisions).
#
# Inputs (the situation when an ACS-layer event is processed;
# j is the process to which the event refers):
#   "kind of ACS event"     { Q(j) = 1, BA_j outputs 0, BA_j outputs 1 }
#   "input to BA_j"         { none, 0, 1 }
#                             - the value, if any, this process
#                               has previously entered to BA_j
#   "post-output BA-output count == n"               { yes, no }
#                             - the total number of BAs that
#                               have output any value, after
#                               this event is recorded
#
# Outputs:
#   "input 1 to BA_j"               { yes, no }
#   "output SubSet"                 { yes, no }
#                                     - SubSet := { j : BA_j had output 1 }
#                                       computed when this output fires
#
# Bracha Phase Retry (BPR) adds its inputs and outputs below, in
# the sub-tables after Step 3: the retry's verdict gate and the
# sweep-side duty trichotomy.  One dispatch serves every site
# (bkr94acsAcastInput, bkr94acsTurn, the retry gate, the duty
# site); each feeds the inputs its own outputs read from state
# and the rest a fixed value, and discards the outputs it does
# not read -- an undecided byte fed by fiat walks, a fanout seam
# fed by fiat is HELD, and both are dropped.  Nothing a site reads
# moves with a value it fed by fiat; that is a fact of the rows,
# checked rather than derived: test_predicates enumerates every
# site's input space against the snippet and requires it.

# ================================================================
# Step 1 - Input 1 to BA_j when Q(j) = 1
# ================================================================

# Rule 1.  When Q(j) = 1 becomes known to me, if I have not
# entered a value to BA_j, input 1 to BA_j.  Bracha's Lemma 3
# (via the underlying reliable broadcast) guarantees that every
# honest process eventually LEARNS Q(j) = 1 if any does.  Whether
# it then inputs 1 is a separate question: BA semantics admit one
# input per process, so the rows below leave BA_j alone once a
# value has been entered -- and Step 2 enters 0 into exactly those
# BAs "for which you haven't entered a value yet" (the paper's
# step 2, quoted in full below), which can reach j first.
@"input 1 to BA_j","kind of ACS event","input to BA_j"
yes,"Q(j) = 1",none
no,"Q(j) = 1",0
no,"Q(j) = 1",1
no,"BA_j outputs 0",
no,"BA_j outputs 1",

# ================================================================
# Step 2 - Input 0 to all unentered BAs at n-t BA outputs of 1
# ================================================================

# Rule 2 -- recorded here, fired from the BPR sweep.
#
# The paper's rule: "Upon completing 2t+1 BA protocols with output
# 1, enter input 0 to all BA protocols for which you haven't entered
# a value yet" (normalized against the scan as BKR94ACS.txt's
# rendering note records).
#
# Enabling evidence, not a moment (header note above).  Fired at
# the instant the count crosses, the fastest n-t processes close
# SubSet against every honest process whose A-Cast is still in
# flight.  Nothing in the model compels firing at enabling; the
# deployment's remedy for unbounded latency is the BPR sweep, so
# the firing lives there, beside the retry/retire decisions that
# already do.
#
# bkr94acsFanoutDuty classifies the decision, derived by scanning
# baDecision[] and entered[] (never stored):
#
#   HELD      - unentered BAs remain and BA-output-1 count < n-t
#               (MET is tested first).  The floor (the paper's is
#               2t+1 -- Implementation Note 15): enter 0 below it
#               and a mass of 0-inputs could force SubSet empty.
#               bkr94acsFanout refuses here whenever called.
#   TOLERANCE - count >= n-t and unentered BAs remain.  Enabled.
#               The caller counts sweeps against its patience and
#               calls bkr94acsFanout when it elapses; the fanout
#               fires only on TOLERANCE and refuses elsewhere --
#               the library holds the safety gate and the caller
#               the pacing, the same shape bkr94acsTurn takes, so
#               an unconditional call is safe, and one on every
#               tick is zero patience, not a firing at enabling.
#               While TOLERANCE holds, the same sweep keeps retrying
#               the delayed processes' A-Casts (BPR gate below:
#               undecided -> retry), so patience is spent on exactly
#               the recovery work that can make the firing
#               unnecessary.
#   MET       - no unentered BAs remain; nothing to give up on.
#               Absorbing: the fanout empties the unentered set, so
#               once-per-instance is structural, carried by the set
#               itself rather than by a latch.
#
# The trichotomy names the one decision shape -- proceed without
# the tail -- under the caller discipline both duty queries share
# (count sweeps only while TOLERANCE; the patience is deployment
# tuning).
#
# Out of the merged dispatch because its inputs (the n-t count,
# the unentered set) appear in no other rule --
# merging would duplicate its DAG at every leaf -- and patience
# is deployment vocabulary, not paper vocabulary.  The trichotomy
# and the firing decision are the BPR sub-tables at the end of this
# file, shared with the round turn below; the enabling predicates
# are plain C at the duty site (bkr94acsDuty in bkr94acs.c) and the
# fanout loop (enter 0 where input is none) in bkr94acsFanout.

# ================================================================
# The BA round turn -- not a BKR94 rule; fired from the BPR sweep
# ================================================================

# The composition's other sweep-side decision.  Bracha's Fig4 steps
# each wait on n-t validations of their own round ("Wait until
# validate n - t (3i+1)-messages", and its 3i+2 and 3i+3
# counterparts) and then compute over that set (the majority at
# 3i+1, the decide/adopt thresholds at 3i+3).  The set keeps
# growing past n-t and Bracha's proofs are read as holding for ANY
# >= n-t sample (BPR.md, The Sweep-Side Decisions) -- so the sample
# a turn consumes is purely a function of WHEN the turn fires.
# Turning a round inside the arrival path, on the delivery that
# completes it, is firing at enabling and consumes the first n-t --
# the same reading Step 2 carries, with a different cost: not
# exclusion (a late round-k message still validates and still
# serves round-k+1 validation) but coin luck.  At 3i+3 the
# decide/adopt counts are monotone thresholds (per-sender dedup:
# counts only grow), so a fuller sample can only convert coin
# phases into deterministic decides; at 3i+1 the majority is a
# comparison a fuller sample can flip -- both outcomes read as
# proof-covered, the flip trading between sound broadcasts, never
# against safety.
#
# The arrival path only BANKS evidence (Fig1 input, Fig3
# store/validate/cascade -- Fig3 itself is deferral-shaped:
# every message is stored and validity re-derived as prior rounds
# grow).  bkr94acsTurnDuty classifies a BA's next round with the
# shared trichotomy of the BPR sub-tables below (HELD below n-t validated or
# round space spent / TOLERANCE at >= n-t with the sample still
# growable / MET at all n validated -- the full sample, firing is
# free), and bkr94acsTurn computes one round under the same caller
# discipline as the fanout.  BA_DECIDED / COMPLETE / BA_EXHAUSTED emerge from
# turns only; the Step 3 dispatch below rides the turn's decide.
#
# bracha87.[hc] carries the caller-shaped seam --
# bracha87Fig3RoundComplete, bracha87Fig3GetValid,
# bracha87Fig4Round -- and WHEN a completed round is turned is this
# composition's choice.

# ================================================================
# Step 3 - Output SubSet when all n BAs have output
# ================================================================

# Rule 3.  Upon any BA_j outputting, if all n BAs have now
# output a value, output SubSet := { j : BA_j had output 1 }.
# BKR94 Lemma 2 Part A guarantees |SubSet| >= 2t+1, and n-t here
# (the two coincide only at n = 3t+1; see Implementation Note 15);
# Part C guarantees cross-process agreement on SubSet; Part D
# guarantees Q(j) = 1 for every j in SubSet.  The "all n BAs"
# threshold can only be reached once per ACS instance (each BA
# outputs at most once), so this rule is naturally idempotent.
#
# Computing the SubSet itself - iterating over the N BAs and
# collecting indices with output 1 - is a C-side loop; the DTC
# fires the go-signal and the C produces the set.
@"output SubSet","kind of ACS event","post-output BA-output count == n"
yes,"BA_j outputs 1",yes
yes,"BA_j outputs 0",yes
no,"BA_j outputs 1",no
no,"BA_j outputs 0",no
no,"Q(j) = 1",

# ================================================================
# Bracha Phase Retry (BPR) -- the composition's rules, as
# sub-tables of this dispatch (BPR.md, The Composition Layer and
# The Sweep-Side Decisions).  No paper behind them.
# ================================================================
#
# Justification for BPR's placement at this layer: end-to-end
# argument (Saltzer/Reed/Clark 1984; see SRC84.txt).  The
# "still owed" predicate for A-Cast-Fig1 retry combines
# Bracha's own retry state (the sent flags and the per-sender
# bitmaps its gates read) with this layer's per-process
# BA-decided state (undecided / decided 0 / decided 1 /
# exhausted); both live at the BKR endpoint, so the retire-or-
# retry decision is made here rather than below.
#
# ===== walk A-Cast j (the verdict gate) =====
#
# bkr94acsRetryStep walks per-process A-Cast Fig1 instances and
# decides whether to output retries based on the local BA
# decision state:
#   - undecided  -> walk  (Q(j)=1 may still be learned by
#                          other honest processes; their Fig1(j)
#                          needs our echoes / readys)
#   - decided 0  -> skip  (j excluded from SubSet at every correct
#                          process, BA agreement, so nobody needs
#                          its A-Cast; our own enter, 0 by Step 2
#                          or 1 by Step 1, is spent)
#   - exhausted  -> walk  (no decision was made; other
#                          processes may still benefit from
#                          earlier-round echoes / readys)
#   - decided 1  -> walk  (Bracha post-decide continuation:
#                          processes that have not yet observed
#                          Fig1 ACCEPT for j still need our
#                          echoes / readys, else their BA_j
#                          stays undecided and may go to 0
#                          via Step 2, violating cross-process
#                          agreement on SubSet)
# The BA Fig 1 walk is ungated; bracha87Fig1Bpr returns 0 on
# unsent instances.
#
# Inputs:
#   "BA_j decision"          { undecided, decided 0, decided 1, exhausted }
# Outputs:
#   "walk A-Cast j"          { yes, no }
@"walk A-Cast j","BA_j decision"
yes,undecided
no,"decided 0"
yes,"decided 1"
yes,exhausted

# ===== the sweep-side duty trichotomy =====
#
# Both seams -- the Step 2 fanout above and the BA round turn --
# take one decision shape, proceed without the tail, classified
# from the seam's own two predicates, computed in C at the duty
# site and bridged:
#   "enabled"                  fanout: BA-output-1 count >= n-t
#                                (the paper's 2t+1; Implementation
#                                Note 15) -- below it entering 0 is
#                                unsound, a mass of 0-inputs could
#                                force SubSet empty;
#                              turn: the BA's next round exists (the
#                                round space is not spent: neither
#                                the ceiling nor a decided BA's
#                                bound) and is
#                                complete at >= n-t validated.
#   "nothing left to wait for" fanout: no BA is unentered -- the set
#                                the firing empties, so once per
#                                instance is structural;
#                              turn: the next round has all n
#                                validated -- the full sample, so
#                                waiting buys nothing.  Only ever yes
#                                with "enabled" yes.
#
# The classification:
#   HELD       not enabled: firing now would be unsound or is
#              impossible; elapsed time is irrelevant -- wait.
#   TOLERANCE  enabled, and waiting could still improve the outcome:
#              the caller counts sweeps against its patience and fires
#              when it elapses.
#   MET        nothing left to wait for.
#
# The seams differ in what MET means, and that is the one reachable
# difference: the turn's MET is a full sample and the firing is free;
# the fanout's MET is an empty unentered set and the firing is moot, so
# it does not fire.  (The cell enabled = no with nothing left = yes is
# MET for the fanout and HELD for the turn; the turn never reaches it.)
# Everywhere else the firing follows the classification: at TOLERANCE
# it fires (the caller decided WHEN by calling); at HELD it refuses,
# having changed nothing.  The library holds the safety half; whether
# to call while TOLERANCE holds is the caller's alone.
#
# For the fanout "nothing left to wait for" decides MET whatever the
# count: a run whose every A-Cast enters 1 by step 1 before n-t BAs
# decide goes from HELD to MET without ever reaching TOLERANCE.
#
# Inputs:
#   "seam"                       { fanout, turn }
#   "enabled"                    { yes, no }
#   "nothing left to wait for"   { yes, no }
# Outputs:
#   "duty"                       { HELD, TOLERANCE, MET }
#   "fire on call"               { yes, no }

@"duty","seam","enabled","nothing left to wait for"
MET,fanout,,yes
TOLERANCE,fanout,yes,no
HELD,fanout,no,no
MET,turn,yes,yes
TOLERANCE,turn,yes,no
HELD,turn,no,

# The fanout at MET has nothing to enter; the turn at MET consumes the
# full sample.  Both refuse at HELD.
@"fire on call","seam","enabled","nothing left to wait for"
no,fanout,,yes
yes,fanout,yes,no
no,fanout,no,no
yes,turn,yes,
no,turn,no,
